Q: DataTables serverSide triggers multiple duplicate AJAX requests in nextjs A: <p>The problem is that DataTables with <code>serverSide: true</code> fires its own AJAX request on mount, <strong>and</strong> your <code>useEffect</code> also triggers <code>ajax.reload()</code>. When you use <code>key={paramsKey}</code>, React destroys and remounts the component, causing DataTables to reinitialize — the 8/16/24 escalation is event listener accumulation.</p>
<p>The fix: use a <strong>stable component</strong> (no <code>key</code>) with controlled reload:</p>
<pre class="lang-tsx prettyprint-override"><code>'use client';
import DataTable from 'datatables.net-react';
import DT from 'datatables.net-dt';
import Responsive from 'datatables.net-responsive-dt';
import { useRef, useEffect, useCallback } from 'react';
import { useSearchParams } from 'next/navigation';

DataTable.use(DT);
DataTable.use(Responsive);

const DashboardTable = () =&gt; {
  const tableRef = useRef&lt;any&gt;(null);
  const searchParams = useSearchParams();

  const fetchData = useCallback(async (data: any, callback: any) =&gt; {
    try {
      const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
      params.set('page', (Math.floor(data.start / data.length) + 1).toString());
      params.set('pageSize', data.length.toString());
      const response = await fetch(`/api/tires?${params.toString()}`);
      const result = await response.json();
      callback({
        draw: data.draw,
        data: result.records || [],
        recordsTotal: result.totalCount || 0,
        recordsFiltered: result.totalCount || 0,
      });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error:', error);
      callback({ draw: data.draw, data: [], recordsTotal: 0, recordsFiltered: 0 });
    }
  }, []);

  const isFirstRender = useRef(true);
  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (isFirstRender.current) {
      isFirstRender.current = false;
      return; // skip — DataTables already fetches on mount
    }
    tableRef.current?.dt()?.ajax.reload(null, false);
  }, [searchParams]);

  return (
    &lt;DataTable ref={tableRef} ajax={fetchData} className=&quot;display nowrap&quot;
      options={{
        responsive: true, processing: true, searching: false,
        serverSide: true, autoWidth: false,
        columns: [
          { data: 'TireId' }, { data: 'VaultName' }, { data: 'Brand' },
          { data: 'Model2' }, { data: 'Size' }, { data: 'Height' },
          { data: 'Width' }, { data: 'LoadIndexId' }, { data: 'speedIndex' },
          { data: 'Patched' }, { data: 'Tread' }, { data: 'DOT' },
        ],
      }}
    &gt;
      &lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;
        &lt;th&gt;ID&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Store&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Brand&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Model&lt;/th&gt;
        &lt;th&gt;Rin&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Aspect&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Width&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Load&lt;/th&gt;
        &lt;th&gt;Speed&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;RFT&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Tread&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;DOT&lt;/th&gt;
      &lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/thead&gt;
    &lt;/DataTable&gt;
  );
};
export default DashboardTable;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why this works:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No <code>key</code> prop</strong> — component stays mounted, DataTables doesn't reinitialize</li>
<li><strong><code>useCallback</code> for fetchData</strong> — always reads current <code>window.location.search</code></li>
<li><strong>Skip first useEffect</strong> — avoids duplicate request since DataTables fetches on init</li>
<li><strong><code>ajax.reload(null, false)</code></strong> — single clean reload, keeps pagination</li>
</ul>

Q: How to write log details to a file using .NET Core 6 A: <p>You can log to file by using &quot;Serilog.Extensions.Logging.File&quot; NuGet:</p>
<ul>
<li>Right click your project and choose Mange NuGet Package.in browse tab, search for &quot;Serilog.Extensions.Logging.File&quot; and install it.</li>
<li>in appsettings.json add the below snippet</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;Logging&quot;: {
    &quot;LogLevel&quot;: {
      &quot;Default&quot;: &quot;Information&quot;,
      &quot;Microsoft.AspNetCore&quot;: &quot;Warning&quot;
    },
    &quot;LogFilePath&quot;: &quot;Logs\\log-{Date}.txt&quot;
  }
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>in Program.cs class, after builder.Build() add the below snippet</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>    var loggerFactory = app.Services.GetService&lt;ILoggerFactory&gt;();
loggerFactory.AddFile(builder.Configuration[&quot;Logging:LogFilePath&quot;].ToString());    
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>build your project and run it. you will find the Logs folder in the root directory of your project.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can log any thing you need in your controller or service, just inject ILogger interface in your constructor as below.</p>
<pre><code> private readonly ILogger&lt;HomeController&gt; _logger;
 public HomeController(ILogger&lt;HomeController&gt; logger)
        {
            _logger = logger;
        }
</code></pre>
<p>Here are examples to log any thing like your process steps, information, warning in any place or exception in catch block. Example of information logging:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>_logger.LogInformation(&quot;API started at:&quot;+DateTime.Now); 
</code></pre>
<p>Example of exception logging in catch block:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>_logger.LogError(ex, ex.Message);
</code></pre>

Q: How to manage a &quot;generic thread&quot; for heavy operations A: <p>The solution is to create a <strong>single Worker Thread</strong> that acts as a database proxy using an RPC (Remote Procedure Call) pattern. The main thread sends messages to the worker with the method name and parameters, and the worker executes the synchronous query (which doesn't block the main thread because it runs in a separate thread) and sends the result back.</p>
<p>Here's a complete implementation:</p>
<p><strong>db-worker.js</strong> (the single worker thread)</p>
<pre><code>js
const { parentPort } = require('worker_threads');
const Database = require('better-sqlite3');

const db = new Database('your-database.db');

// Register all your service methods here
const services = {
  getUsers: () =&gt; db.prepare('SELECT * FROM users').all(),
  getUserById: (id) =&gt; db.prepare('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?').get(id),
  insertUser: (name, email) =&gt; db.prepare('INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)').run(name, email),
  // Add all your 10+ service methods here...
};

parentPort.on('message', ({ id, method, params }) =&gt; {
  try {
    const result = services[method](...(params || []));
    parentPort.postMessage({ id, result });
  } catch (error) {
    parentPort.postMessage({ id, error: error.message });
  }
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>db-proxy.js</strong> (used in the main/renderer process)</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const { Worker } = require('worker_threads');
const path = require('path');

class DbProxy {
  constructor() {
    this.worker = new Worker(path.join(__dirname, 'db-worker.js'));
    this.callbacks = new Map();
    this.nextId = 0;

    this.worker.on('message', ({ id, result, error }) =&gt; {
      const cb = this.callbacks.get(id);
      if (cb) {
        this.callbacks.delete(id);
        if (error) cb.reject(new Error(error));
        else cb.resolve(result);
      }
    });
  }

  call(method, ...params) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {
      const id = this.nextId++;
      this.callbacks.set(id, { resolve, reject });
      this.worker.postMessage({ id, method, params });
    });
  }

  async close() {
    await this.worker.terminate();
  }
}

module.exports = new DbProxy(); // singleton
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Usage in your Electron main process or renderer:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const db = require('./db-proxy');

// All calls are now async and non-blocking
const users = await db.call('getUsers');
const user = await db.call('getUserById', 42);
await db.call('insertUser', 'John', 'john@example.com');
</code></pre>
<p>This pattern gives you a single thread for all DB operations while keeping your main process responsive. Every service method is just an entry in the <code>services</code> object in the worker.</p>
<p>If you want something more automated, check out the <strong><a href="https://github.com/GoogleChromeLabs/comlink" rel="nofollow noreferrer">comlink</a></strong> library which can automatically proxy class methods over Worker Threads with minimal boilerplate.</p>
